<h1>Orcraft Setup Overview</h1>
<p>This is how a typical Orcraft setup looks like.</p>

{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/Diagrams/Orcraft-Setup.png", "Orcraft setup diagram: controller connected to an external network and to internal endpoints and a CI runner") }}

<p>A typical Orcraft deployment consists of a single controller and endpoints. The
  controller orchestrates test execution, distributes the test package and collects results,
  while each endpoint receives the package and executes the assigned steps. Endpoints are
  registered with the controller prior to use.</p>

<p>A CI runner is not an endpoint: it is a separate controller installation running in CLI mode,
  using the same configuration exported from the original controller as a CI snapshot. This
  allows a pipeline to reproduce a run exactly as it was configured, without a GUI.</p>

<p>The same controller can be used for test development, manual execution and as a CI runner, or
  these roles can be split across separate controller installations, each dedicated to a single
  function. In either case, controllers can share endpoints between them, allowing the same
  infrastructure to serve development, manual and CI workflows.</p>

<p>Endpoints may also be reached over an external network, allowing distributed teams and remote
  labs to participate in the same session. Communication between the controller and its endpoints
  is secured with mutual TLS and token-based authentication, ensuring that connections beyond the
  internal network remain safe.</p>

<p>Endpoints are not a strict requirement. The controller can execute structured tests on
  localhost, making it possible to develop and run suites on a single machine before extending
  execution to additional endpoints.</p>

